course: dictionary
lesson: 13539
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can find out which meaning the writer wants by reading the words around it. Imposition is one of these words. «I hope it's not an imposition if I stay for tea» is polite talk: it means I hope I am not asking too much of your kindness.
explain: Now read this: «The king's new imposition of a tax angered the town.» This imposition is a rule or tax put on people. The clue is the word tax, and the sentence is about a king, not about asking a favour. Always check: does my meaning fit this sentence?
explain: Some words that sound grown-up have simple meanings. «Ella had to write an imposition after school for talking in class.» In old-fashioned UK schools, an imposition was an extra piece of written work given as a punishment. The clues are after school and talking in class, which show it is a punishment.
fact: Imposition is a noun, and its plural is impositions.
fact: «Imposition» means an unfair or unwelcome demand on someone's time, help or kindness.
fact: «Imposition» can also mean making a rule, tax or punishment apply to people.
fact: In old-fashioned UK schools, an imposition was an extra piece of written work given as a punishment.
fact: Phrases to know: an imposition on someone, the imposition of a tax, and not an imposition, which means it is no bother.
exercise: «I hope it's not an imposition if I stay for tea.» What does imposition mean here? | A happy surprise | A tax the government puts on people | *Asking too much of someone's kindness | An extra piece of writing for school
exercise: «The government's imposition of a new tax made many families angry.» What does imposition mean here? | *A rule or tax put on people | A short holiday | A pleasant gift | A kind offer of help
exercise: «Ella had to write an imposition for talking in class.» In this sentence, an imposition is... | A party invitation | A kind favour from a friend | *An extra piece of writing given as a punishment | A map of the school
test: «Would it be an imposition if I borrowed your bike for the afternoon?» In this question, imposition means... | A new law about bikes | A prize for good riding | *An unwelcome demand on your kindness | A safe place to park
test: «The teacher gave Sam an imposition: fifty lines to copy out.» What is the imposition? | A kind invitation to lunch | A prize for good work | *An extra piece of written work given as a punishment | A trip to the library
exam: «Please don't think it is an imposition if I ask for one more favour.» What does imposition mean? | A loud noise in the street | A type of school prize | A new law for the whole town | *An unwelcome demand on someone's kindness
